On Friday 09 September 2005 17:06, Jorge Llambías wrote:
> But for days of the week things are more confusing. Not
> everybody agrees which day is day 1. I believe Russian
> and Portuguese for example both use numbers for the
> names of the days, but they don't agree on where to start
> the count.

Portuguese, Greek, and Hebrew agree that Monday is the second day, so this can 
be taken to be the Yahwist opinion (especially because of the Greek and 
Hebrew). Russian considers Tuesday to be second, but Wednesday middle. The 
word for Saturday in all these is or is derived from Shabbat. The Russian 
words have arbitrary suffixes.

pt: domingo, segunda-feira, terça-feira, quarta-feira, quinta-feira, 
sexta-feira, sábado
el: kuriaké, deutera, trité, tetarté, pempté, paraskeué, sabbato
he: yom rishon, yom sheni, yom shlishi, yom rvi`i, yom chamishi, yom shishi, 
shabbat
ru: voskresenye, ponedel'nik, vtornik, sreda, chetverg, pyatnitsa, subbota

Galician, however, which is sometimes considered a dialect of Portuguese or 
vice versa, uses the Roman gods as do other Romance languages: domingo, luns, 
martes, mércores, xoves, venres, sábado.

